{"name":"Blue Titmouse","hex":"#4466ff","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue titmouse","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of blue tit (Cyanistes caeruleus)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue_titmouse"}],"entries":[{"title":"Baeolophus","blocks":[{"text":"Baeolophus is a genus of birds in the family Paridae, commonly called tits. Its members are known as titmouses or titmice. All the species are native to North America. In the past, most authorities retained Baeolophus as a subgenus within the genus Parus, but treatment as a distinct genus, initiated by the American Ornithological Society, is now widely accepted."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Tufted_Titmouse-27527-2.jpg/330px-Tufted_Titmouse-27527-2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baeolophus","wikidata":"Q788397"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Bridled_Titmouse_%28Baeolophus_wollweberi%29_%2816875140301%29.jpg/500px-Bridled_Titmouse_%28Baeolophus_wollweberi%29_%2816875140301%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Bridled_Titmouse_%28Baeolophus_wollweberi%29_%2816875140301%29.jpg/1280px-Bridled_Titmouse_%28Baeolophus_wollweberi%29_%2816875140301%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Bridled_Titmouse_%28Baeolophus_wollweberi%29_%2816875140301%29.jpg/500px-Bridled_Titmouse_%28Baeolophus_wollweberi%29_%2816875140301%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABridled_Titmouse_(Baeolophus_wollweberi)_(16875140301).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Baeolophus_inornatus_-San_Luis_Obispo%2C_California%2C_USA-8.jpg/500px-Baeolophus_inornatus_-San_Luis_Obispo%2C_California%2C_USA-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Baeolophus_inornatus_-San_Luis_Obispo%2C_California%2C_USA-8.jpg/500px-Baeolophus_inornatus_-San_Luis_Obispo%2C_California%2C_USA-8.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABaeolophus_inornatus_-San_Luis_Obispo%2C_California%2C_USA-8.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Baeolophus_ridgwayi_Arizona.jpg/500px-Baeolophus_ridgwayi_Arizona.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Baeolophus_ridgwayi_Arizona.jpg/1280px-Baeolophus_ridgwayi_Arizona.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Baeolophus_ridgwayi_Arizona.j